    # ===========================
    # HAND TO MOUTH SUBSYSTEM
    # ===========================

    # source stat block (page 107)
    #XKni5bVXWOguZdO6SYfVhh ^"Hand to Mouth System" DEF {
        APPLIES TO [#t9446c0a7f578c8eaf296f ^"System"]
        PROPERTIES {
            ^"Description" STRING "Economic subsystem for ronin characters reflecting the challenges of living without a lord's resources. Covers trade in Rokugan, making money, fluctuating currency values, travel papers, and the slow erosion of status and honor that comes with lordless survival."
        }
        RULES {
            #L5RPW09oZ4mZ0zK7nU0hP3lL5iI: trade_philosophy "Trade in Rokugan is considered beneath a samurai's station. This is openly acknowledged by the lorded samurai, who look down upon anyone who resorts to basic commerce. One reason for this is the Empire's embrace of Shinseism, which teaches that material things are spiritually staining and that obsession with money and commerce is a distraction from spiritual development. Desire is the root of all suffering, the Tao of Shinsei teaches, and what is money but the objectification—the worship—of desire itself?\n\nAmong the kuge and most of the buke—the aristocratic and samurai classes––barter is the most common form of trade. Money, especially coin, almost never changes hands. Their system is one of gifts and favors. This is considered far cleaner than simple trade with coin, which is for the lower castes, not honorable samurai. Coin is backed by goods, but barter is backed by honor and a warrior's word. When a harvest is bad, the minted coin of those lands loses value, but the honor of the lord is still worth much, and there is always some thing to trade.\n\nBecause rōnin are considered samurai, all of this applies to them as well. But it gets complicated, because the reality of their situation contradicts the role of trade in their lives. Rōnin have nothing supplied for them. They have no lord to pay for everything. A life of hand-to-mouth hardship has made them far more sensitive to the costs of things. They must spend a large portion of their time considering, planning, and preparing to work, trade with coin, and even steal to get by––and such desperate and unclean practices is far beneath a lorded samurai. Rōnin also barter for favors and with their word, if they are honorable enough for others to trust them, but simple barter often isn't enough to sustain a lordless samurai."
            #L5RPW09yV9gS7dH1eE2fF5kE5iA: ronin_economics "Rōnin are subject to financial forces beyond their control. Rokugan has no unified currency, and the value of any type of coin (zeni, bu, or koku) in a particular region may fluctuate depending on how good the harvest was or how much the clan issuing the coin is spending (or saving) to further an upcoming campaign. Lorded samurai, sheltered by their feudal ties, can ignore this fluctuation in value. Rōnin cannot.\n\nFluctuations in value are almost universally a peasant concern. Merchant samurai exist, but they fill a specific niche required by their clan and have spent generations establishing the means to make financial systems work to their and their clan's advantage. But a rōnin, who relies on money to survive, may discover that their hard-earned coin, which they risked their life to obtain, no longer buys what it did two seasons ago. Or worse, it may not be taken at all."
            #L5RPW09bE2nP4mH5gP5fT3oD5rY: weapon_acquisition "Additionally, many things a rōnin requires simply aren't for sale. For instance, weapons are not carried at any shop. Katana and other weapons are commissioned by lords on an as-needed basis, because the length, weight, and balance of a sword should be tailored to the size and preferences of the wielder. If a rōnin requires a new sword, they must convince a blacksmith (who may be forbidden by the region's clan to make weapons for people outside that clan) to forge one for them.\n\nMore commonly, the rōnin will simply ambush a samurai in a dark alley, kill them, and take their sword. This is much easier, albeit grossly dishonorable, and doubtless will result in severe consequences. However, as time passes, rōnin grow desperate and more willing to sacrifice honor in order to survive."
            #L5RPW09qO3bT0sC6tG7gF1bZ7aC: travel_papers "So how does a rōnin get travel papers? A person can only travel the Empire with the explicit permission of those who maintain the roads, and that requires travel papers. Most papers only permit travel on a specific road, to a specific location, for a specific purpose. For clan samurai, obtaining these papers is trivial. But rōnin have no lord and are not in good standing. For them, it is much more difficult.\n\nIf rōnin wish to obtain travel papers, it means humbling themselves before those who can grant them. It means doing favors for magistrates, even if the tasks are demeaning. It means working for free, if work is offered at all.\n\nWithout papers, rōnin roam the Empire at great risk. If they are caught crossing a border without travel papers, they might be assumed to be spies or bandits. Former clan samurai may have the travel papers they received when they were expelled from their clan (a courtesy offered to facilitate their expeditious dismissal), but such papers quickly expire. Peasants who choose to masquerade as rōnin may be able to get travel papers (for instance, if they are merchants or traders), but they have difficulty crossing borders if they are illiterate or are dressed as peasants or rōnin while they do so. Unauthorized travel is illegal, so a rōnin caught this way is at the mercy of their captors. It is much safer for rōnin to restrict themselves to a specific region so these problems don't come up."
            #L5RPW09hR9gI7cK1sV0xM4wZ7rF: lifestyle_degradation "A rōnin who is still presenting themself as a lorded samurai lives a higher-maintenance lifestyle than a peasant. If a rōnin doesn't have access to a lord's resources, who will repair their sword? Who will maintain their armor from battle to battle? Who will care for their expensive pony, or clean their silk kimono? These things cost money, assuming the rōnin can procure such services at all. Many find it simpler to sell off their fine things, so they no longer have to worry about them. The pony is usually the first to be sold, followed by the armor. Desperate rōnin will even sell off their weapons, although usually for scrap metal, as peasants are forbidden from wielding them. Such sales are usually to a rōnin's disadvantage, as they do not know what these things are worth and bartering is beneath many of them. In a life cast to the waves, it is the pragmatic who prosper when what money does best is \"run out.\""
        }
    }

    # ===========================
    # CELESTIAL IMPLEMENTS SYSTEM
    # ===========================

    #dXJtqxf1BdvNh2FeLMb4y3 ^"Celestial Implements System" DEF {
        APPLIES TO [#t9446c0a7f578c8eaf296f ^"System"]
        PROPERTIES {
            ^"Name" STRING "Celestial Implements (Astras)"
            ^"Source" STRING "Ivory Kingdoms tradition"
            ^"Description" STRING "Celestial implements are divine weapons and objects invoked through sacred mantras. An astradhari (wielder of astras) must learn the Summoning Mantra for each specific implement. Implements have a Blessing Source (deity, element, counter element, restriction), a Form (projectile, melee, armor, utility), and Boons (supernatural qualities)."
        }
        RULES {
            #L5RPW06vH3gV1uZ4uQ6vA6sG3hP: implement_summoning_limit "Note that the Summoning Mantra: [Name of Implement] technique exists independently for each celestial implement. Each must be purchased independently to gain access to a specific implement (i.e., Summoning Mantra: Banhi's Thunderous Astra only ever grants access to that specific weapon, and it must be learned separately from Summoning Mantra: River Stone of Flowing Water, which calls forth a different distinct item). A character may learn a number of specific summoning mantras equal to their school rank (provided they have access to a sage who knows those mantras—and is willing to teach them)."
            #L5RPW06pB8nH1fI6iT8wC6gD8iP: implement_staked_honor "Then, you must stake honor equal to two times your honor rank on upholding the implement's restriction while you have the implement (see page 99)."
            #L5RPW06uE1lU0wN9oB8iF5vP9sR: implement_restriction_break "If you break the implement's restriction during this time, you lose your staked honor and the implement is dispelled. You cannot summon this implement again this scene."
            #L5RPW06bG1cK1gB3aF7pF2xY2qA: implement_qualities "A celestial implement has the same profile and provides the same benefits as its mundane equivalent, with the modifications listed for its form in this section. Additionally, it gains the Ceremonial, Resplendent, and Sacred qualities (losing Mundane and Subtle, if the item had those qualities)."
        }
    }

    # ===========================
    # SCHOOL-BUILDING GUIDELINES
    # ===========================

    # The book provides a detailed school-building system in
    # Chapter 2 (pages 76-84). Key reference tables are encoded here.

    # source stat block (page 76)
    #PcDstLTCy3kkdx8YMD1vXi ^"School Building System" DEF {
        APPLIES TO [#t9446c0a7f578c8eaf296f ^"System"]
        PROPERTIES {
            ^"Description" STRING "Step-by-step system for creating custom schools. Steps: 1) Determine role(s), 2) Choose affiliations and summary, 3) Design school ability, 4) Choose ring bonuses, 5) Choose skills, 6) Select technique access and starting techniques, 7) Build curriculum and mastery ability, 8) Starting outfit, 9) Name."
        }
        # source table (page 76). The seven roles a school can take; the role(s)
        # drive skills, techniques, and ring bonuses in the steps that follow.
        #L5RPW07i2cs9XlGP54LtGz ^"Table 2-3: Role Breakdown" DEF {
            ENTRIES {
                #L5RPW07Po5dTIUNMx2JfR3 ^"Artisan" DEF  { ^"Description" STRING "Creators, advisors, engineers, and scholars of art" }
                #L5RPW07ue5cw9G3jfaSs40 ^"Bushi" DEF    { ^"Description" STRING "Warriors, guards, and instructors" }
                #L5RPW07NVHxK9f1ssqLmaq ^"Courtier" DEF { ^"Description" STRING "Politicians, emissaries, and civil servants" }
                #L5RPW07XArmniV4mCqtHlz ^"Monk" DEF     { ^"Description" STRING "Political influencers, traveling historians, pinnacles of monastic life and (supposedly) above earthly concerns" }
                #L5RPW07v79w3pAKi3tBH5h ^"Sage" DEF     { ^"Description" STRING "Those who wield supernatural powers differently from or beyond how a shugenja might wield them in Rokugan" }
                #L5RPW079cQrnWSb3ocMsE0 ^"Shinobi" DEF  { ^"Description" STRING "Assassins and spies" }
                #L5RPW07IN5rid5LqVwyhU4 ^"Shugenja" DEF { ^"Description" STRING "Priests of the samurai caste or others who have a special bond with the kami or can control them" }
            }
        }

        # source table (pages 78-79). School-ability templates by role; two are open to Any role.
        #L5RPW07eCo27KwuQzU6dMD ^"Table 2-4: Generic School Abilities" DEF {
            ENTRIES {
                #L5RPW07XZ3OIYxS8bxbATk ^"School Ability Template 1" DEF { ^"Role" STRING "Any" ^"Ability Template" STRING "Choose one skill group when you create this school ability. When you perform a check using a skill from that group, you may treat your ranks in the skill as being equal to your school rank. If your ranks in that skill are equal to or higher than your school rank, or if you have 5 ranks in the skill, you may add one kept ■ set to an ⚙ result instead." }
                #L5RPW07ZT6hUD2vM6Pbpuu ^"School Ability Template 2" DEF { ^"Role" STRING "Any" ^"Ability Template" STRING "Choose one skill when you create this school ability. Once per scene, when you make a check using this skill, you may add a number of kept ■ set to ⚙ results equal to your school rank." }
                #L5RPW076LZqQNaU60H1jyU ^"School Ability Template 3" DEF { ^"Role" STRING "Artisan" ^"Ability Template" STRING "Choose one Artisan skill when you create this school ability. Before you make a check using that skill, you may receive strife up to your school rank to reduce the TN by that amount." }
                #L5RPW07hlRuqkugL8LrF2W ^"School Ability Template 4" DEF { ^"Role" STRING "Bushi" ^"Ability Template" STRING "Choose one weapon type or unarmed when you create this school ability. When you make a skill check using this weapon type, if you succeed, you may receive fatigue up to your school rank to add that many bonus successes." }
                #L5RPW077sMf2v31gHgi3XE ^"School Ability Template 5" DEF { ^"Role" STRING "Courtier" ^"Ability Template" STRING "Choose one Scholar or Social skill when you create this school ability. When you make a check using that skill, you may negate a number of ⚈ symbols up to your school rank." }
                #L5RPW07v2ggGnhBXmzmOva ^"School Ability Template 6" DEF { ^"Role" STRING "Monk" ^"Ability Template" STRING "Choose an action type (Attack, Movement, Scheme, or Support) when you create this school ability. When you make a check to perform an action of this type, before rolling, you may exchange a number of ■ up to your school rank for ⚈. When you provide assistance on an action of this type, the character you assist may exchange one ■ for an ⚈." }
                #L5RPW070gItoRac4gDLUYX ^"School Ability Template 7" DEF { ^"Role" STRING "Sage" ^"Ability Template" STRING "Choose one Artisan or Scholar skill when you create this school ability. You may use this skill for invocations, and you cannot channel invocations (see pages 189–190 of the core rulebook). Once per scene, when you succeed on a check to use an invocation technique, you may receive a number of strife up to your school rank to add that many bonus successes." }
                #L5RPW07fHyuiRAqPMT1wa3 ^"School Ability Template 8" DEF { ^"Role" STRING "Shinobi" ^"Ability Template" STRING "Choose one Social or Trade skill when you create this school ability. Once per scene when you make a check using that skill, you may choose a number of dropped dice up to your school rank and reserve them. Until the end of the scene, when you make a check using a different skill, you may add one of the reserved dice to the check as a kept die, set to the result it had when it was reserved. This expends that reserved die." }
                #L5RPW07G979MHKgYwve65y ^"School Ability Template 9" DEF { ^"Role" STRING "Shugenja" ^"Ability Template" STRING "Choose an effect keyword (Augment, Bind, Curse, Mend, Purify, Scry, Smite, Summon, etc.; see pages 192 and 225 of the core rulebook) when you create this school ability. When you make a check for an action that uses that keyword, you may choose a number of your rolled dice up to your school rank containing ⚈ symbols and reroll them." }
            }
        }

        # source table (page 79). Suggested first ring increase by the school's primary role.
        #L5RPW07SxReWDu92zh6ZKP ^"Table 2-5: Suggested First Ring Bonuses" DEF {
            ENTRIES {
                #L5RPW07tnXomvsgPe41iEP ^"Artisan" DEF { ^"Ring Increase" STRING "Fire or Water" }
                #L5RPW07tJzGM73QLQwX1zV ^"Bushi" DEF { ^"Ring Increase" STRING "Earth" }
                #L5RPW07OT3nHlAxOQvgQ7e ^"Courtier or Shinobi" DEF { ^"Ring Increase" STRING "Air" }
                #L5RPW07TT4IspGuYqvJIJS ^"Monk or Sage" DEF { ^"Ring Increase" STRING "Void" }
                #L5RPW07NaXltjV9CIWez8c ^"Shugenja" DEF { ^"Ring Increase" STRING "The element the school is attuned to" }
            }
        }

        # source table (page 79). Suggested second ring increase by what the school is known for.
        #L5RPW07IGE9GL53SPJKXE3 ^"Table 2-6: Suggested Second Ring Bonus" DEF {
            # each row names the core's ring (core-traits) — the table lists them, it does not define them
            TABLE {
                COLUMNS ["Ring Increase", "Trait School Is Known For"]
                ROW [#L5Rring0Air00000000001 ^"Air", "Precision, grace, or manners"]
                ROW [#L5Rring0Earth000000001 ^"Earth", "Patience, tradition, or resilience"]
                ROW [#L5Rring0Fire0000000001 ^"Fire", "Inventiveness, ferocity, or speed"]
                ROW [#L5Rring0Void0000000001 ^"Void", "Philosophy, selflessness, or insight"]
                ROW [#L5Rring0Water000000001 ^"Water", "Adaptability, flexibility, or awareness"]
            }
        }

        # source table (page 80). Per role: how many skills the school makes available, how many a player picks, and the common skills for that role.
        #L5RPW07mWtzRR0sulWuz9V ^"Table 2-7: Skill Choices" DEF {
            ENTRIES {
                #L5RPW07VR7YdrYo5kdf6wO ^"Artisan" DEF { ^"Skills Available" INTEGER 7 ^"Skill Picks" INTEGER 5 ^"Common Skills Available" STRING "Aesthetics, Composition, Courtesy, Culture, Design, Martial Arts [Melee], Smithing" }
                #L5RPW07f38aQRu7EKHUZbW ^"Bushi" DEF { ^"Skills Available" INTEGER 7 ^"Skill Picks" INTEGER 5 ^"Common Skills Available" STRING "Command, Fitness, Government, Martial Arts [Melee], Martial Arts [Ranged], Martial Arts [Unarmed], Meditation, Tactics" }
                #L5RPW07IiSLzLM17Ry0LZP ^"Courtier" DEF { ^"Skills Available" INTEGER 7 ^"Skill Picks" INTEGER 5 ^"Common Skills Available" STRING "Composition, Courtesy, Culture, Government, Martial Arts [Ranged], Performance, Sentiment" }
                #L5RPW07ubZYC7CiWvLmaD2 ^"Monk" DEF { ^"Skills Available" INTEGER 6 ^"Skill Picks" INTEGER 4 ^"Common Skills Available" STRING "Fitness, Martial Arts [Melee], Martial Arts [Unarmed], Meditation, Survival, Theology" }
                #L5RPW07Qsj9ghNTDBmfEiO ^"Sage" DEF { ^"Skills Available" INTEGER 6 ^"Skill Picks" INTEGER 3 ^"Common Skills Available" STRING "Courtesy, Games, Labor, Meditation, Survival, Theology" }
                #L5RPW07wagxJMEqbyxgQZ8 ^"Shinobi" DEF { ^"Skills Available" INTEGER 7 ^"Skill Picks" INTEGER 5 ^"Common Skills Available" STRING "Courtesy, Culture, Skulduggery, Martial Arts [Melee], Martial Arts [Ranged], Medicine, Performance" }
                #L5RPW07y4dqpoTxwo85Zin ^"Shugenja" DEF { ^"Skills Available" INTEGER 6 ^"Skill Picks" INTEGER 3 ^"Common Skills Available" STRING "Composition, Courtesy, Culture, Games, Meditation, Theology" }
            }
        }

        # source table (page 81). Number of starting techniques by primary role.
        #L5RPW074j65P0EmWG9h2AR ^"Table 2-8: Starting Techniques" DEF {
            ENTRIES {
                #L5RPW07WFUmOnL2bvHwJiE ^"Artisan" DEF { ^"Number of Starting Techniques" STRING "3" }
                #L5RPW071SoJR4AhrkSkzcz ^"Bushi" DEF { ^"Number of Starting Techniques" STRING "2" }
                #L5RPW07dyEWFG3ZgHe8A4d ^"Courtier" DEF { ^"Number of Starting Techniques" STRING "2" }
                #L5RPW07MgnAlVMDmWXFRg4 ^"Monk" DEF { ^"Number of Starting Techniques" STRING "3" }
                #L5RPW07ccis8eLKtiSoFMI ^"Sage" DEF { ^"Number of Starting Techniques" STRING "4–5" }
                #L5RPW076b5H2He75uQJcQf ^"Shinobi" DEF { ^"Number of Starting Techniques" STRING "2" }
                #L5RPW07KAjxqqAAt1vM2ig ^"Shugenja" DEF { ^"Number of Starting Techniques" STRING "4–5 (including the Commune with Spirits Ritual ⛩)" }
            }
        }

        # source table (page 82). The worked example of a rank's seven advances (one skill group, three skills, one technique group, two techniques).
        #L5RPW07zIl3BYkF4Rpq7MU ^"Table 2-9: Morgan's Rank 1 Sample Curriculum" DEF {
            # skills and a technique the core defines are named by the core's hash; the groups are not
            # entities; "Snapping Branch Style" is printed so (the technique on p. 90 is "Snapping Branch Strike")
            TABLE {
                COLUMNS ["Advance", "Type", "Category"]
                ROW ["Artisan Skills", "Skl. Grp.", ""]
                ROW [#L5Rskill0Courtesy00001 ^"Courtesy", "Skill", ""]
                ROW [#L5Rskill0Theology00001 ^"Theology", "Skill", ""]
                ROW [#Nz42rDSYYoVFWHst6Mu3QY ^"Martial Arts [Melee]", "Skill", ""]
                ROW ["Rank 1 Shūji", "Tech. Grp.", "✎"]
                ROW [#YRrwJlgAyPpVIMmg9hKPC4 ^"Civility Foremost", "Technique", "✎"]
                ROW ["Snapping Branch Style", "Technique", "⚔"]
            }
        }

        # source table (page 83). Mastery-ability templates; six are open to Any role, the rest are role-gated.
        #L5RPW07NQztp4lvRkiwxbC ^"Table 2-10: Generic Mastery Abilities" DEF {
            ENTRIES {
                #L5RPW070bq0qNIgQn04FBg ^"Mastery Ability Template 1" DEF { ^"Role" STRING "Any" ^"Ability Template" STRING "Increase your endurance by your ranks in Fitness." }
                #L5RPW07L6pRbXI4VdBpFpg ^"Mastery Ability Template 2" DEF { ^"Role" STRING "Any" ^"Ability Template" STRING "Increase your composure by your ranks in Meditation." }
                #L5RPW07oyd12IE3UBtD5hN ^"Mastery Ability Template 3" DEF { ^"Role" STRING "Any" ^"Ability Template" STRING "Choose one technique category the school does not already have available (kata, kihō, invocations, shūji, etc.) when you gain this mastery ability. That technique type becomes available to you." }
                #L5RPW07q2Jmqt3ARowjEE7 ^"Mastery Ability Template 4" DEF { ^"Role" STRING "Any" ^"Ability Template" STRING "If your school ability can be used a limited number of times per scene, you may spend 1 Void point to use it one additional time per scene." }
                #L5RPW072gf2u0Ilw3PkSUe ^"Mastery Ability Template 5" DEF { ^"Role" STRING "Any" ^"Ability Template" STRING "If your school ability requires you to choose something from a list (a skill, a skill group, a technique category, etc.), choose one additional entry from that list when you create this mastery ability. Your school ability applies to both chosen entries." }
                #L5RPW07gA6limF3kHQnghI ^"Mastery Ability Template 6" DEF { ^"Role" STRING "Any" ^"Ability Template" STRING "Choose one skill when you create this mastery ability. Once per game session when you make a check using that skill, you may reroll all of your dice. If you do not succeed after rerolling, gain 1 Void point." }
                #L5RPW07qyG8YNQH94647V6 ^"Mastery Ability Template 7" DEF { ^"Role" STRING "Monk, Sage, Shugenja" ^"Ability Template" STRING "When you defend against damage, you may treat your physical resistance and supernatural resistance as equal to your ranks in Theology." }
                #L5RPW07Y5ty6fbGXo2A8ng ^"Mastery Ability Template 8" DEF { ^"Role" STRING "Monk, Sage, Shugenja" ^"Ability Template" STRING "Choose one ring when you create this mastery ability. You may train that ring to rank 6 (paying 18 XP)." }
                #L5RPW07C8rcgwkbv1UpjKj ^"Mastery Ability Template 9" DEF { ^"Role" STRING "Artisan, Bushi, Courtier, Shinobi" ^"Ability Template" STRING "Choose one skill group when you choose this mastery ability. You may train skills in that group to rank 6 (paying 12 XP per skill raised from rank 5 to rank 6 this way)." }
                #L5RPW07QpgwCqjBtsuX3g7 ^"Mastery Ability Template 10" DEF { ^"Role" STRING "Artisan, Bushi, Courtier, Shinobi" ^"Ability Template" STRING "Choose one technique on the curriculum that requires a check to activate when you create this mastery ability. You treat the TN of this check as reduced by your ranks in the skill to activate it (to a minimum of TN 1)." }
                #L5RPW071ashSJOJTKH7FgP ^"Mastery Ability Template 11" DEF { ^"Role" STRING "Artisan, Bushi, Courtier, Shinobi" ^"Ability Template" STRING "Choose one technique on the curriculum that requires spending one or more ⚙ to activate when you create this mastery ability. When you spend at least the minimum amount of ⚙ to activate that technique, you count as spending that amount of ⚙ plus ⚙⚙ instead." }
            }
        }

        # source table (page 84). Standard starting equipment by role. (“journel” in the Monk row is reproduced as printed.)
        #L5RPW07T9XAj3Z7QDnHALb ^"Table 2-11: Suggested Starting Outfits" DEF {
            ENTRIES {
                #L5RPW07g69AkOi7Nvip69d ^"Artisan" DEF { ^"Clothing and Armor" STRING "Traveling clothes, ceremonial clothes, and ashigaru armor" ^"Weapons" STRING "Wakizashi (short sword) and yumi (bow) with a quiver of arrows" ^"Other Gear" STRING "Traveling pack, scroll satchel, calligraphy set or musical instrument, journal" }
                #L5RPW07KS9OEVcgUPjQyK8 ^"Bushi" DEF { ^"Clothing and Armor" STRING "Traveling clothes, choice of ashigaru armor or lacquered armor" ^"Weapons" STRING "Daishō (katana and wakizashi); choice of yari (spear), yumi (bow) and a quiver of arrows, or naginata (polearm)" ^"Other Gear" STRING "Traveling pack" }
                #L5RPW07VfGknrdAsv6TVy3 ^"Courtier" DEF { ^"Clothing and Armor" STRING "Traveling clothes, ceremonial clothes" ^"Weapons" STRING "Daishō (katana and wakizashi) and yumi (bow) with a quiver of arrows" ^"Other Gear" STRING "Traveling pack, calligraphy set, scroll satchel" }
                #L5RPW07ErZmXzyZkqvrNXk ^"Monk" DEF { ^"Clothing and Armor" STRING "Traveling clothes" ^"Weapons" STRING "Daishō (katana and wakizashi) and 1 weapon of rarity 6 or lower" ^"Other Gear" STRING "Traveling pack, journel" }
                #L5RPW07h1PpFSxsU2DSmE6 ^"Sage" DEF { ^"Clothing and Armor" STRING "Traveling clothes, choice of sanctified robes or concealed armor" ^"Weapons" STRING "Wakizashi (short sword) and 1 weapon of rarity 5 or lower" ^"Other Gear" STRING "Traveling pack, scroll satchel, calligraphy set, various vials and components" }
                #L5RPW07JwdrUBpY020zhtD ^"Shinobi" DEF { ^"Clothing and Armor" STRING "Traveling clothes, concealed armor, and mundane clothes" ^"Weapons" STRING "Daishō (katana and wakizashi) and 1 weapon of rarity 7 or lower" ^"Other Gear" STRING "Traveling pack, two knives, makeup kit, one vial of poison" }
                #L5RPW07IQuDQssKwuOJGea ^"Shugenja" DEF { ^"Clothing and Armor" STRING "Traveling clothes and sanctified robes" ^"Weapons" STRING "Wakizashi (short sword); choice of yumi (bow) with a quiver of arrows or a bō (staff)" ^"Other Gear" STRING "Traveling pack, scroll satchel, calligraphy set, knife, gifts for the kami" }
            }
        }
    }
}
